# date — real-world examples

## Timestamp suitable for file names

```sh
date +"%Y%m%d_%H%M%S"
# 20260707_063000
```

## Convert a Unix timestamp to a readable date

```sh
date -d @1440359821
```

## Current time as a Unix timestamp

```sh
date +%s
```

## Date arithmetic (GNU date)

```sh
date -d "yesterday"
date -d "next friday"
date -d "2 weeks ago" +%Y-%m-%d
```

## Homelab: timestamped backup archive

```sh
tar czf "cheatsheets-$(date +%Y%m%d).tar.gz" ~/Public/ireaneus-cheatsheets
```

## Homelab: how long since a service restarted

```sh
systemctl show plexmediaserver --property=ActiveEnterTimestamp
```

## UTC and ISO 8601

```sh
date -u                # current UTC time
date -Iseconds         # 2026-07-07T06:30:00-05:00
```

| Format | Meaning              |
| ------ | -------------------- |
| %Y%m%d | 20260707             |
| %H%M%S | 063000               |
| %s     | Unix timestamp       |
| %A     | weekday name         |
| -d     | parse/shift a date   |
| -u     | UTC                  |
